      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i keep getting this annoying SettingWithCopyWarning in my notebook when i try to add a new column to a dataframe i sliced earlier. like i did df2 = df[df['age'] > 30] then df2['status'] = 'adult' but it keeps throwing the warning. what is actually happening and how do i fix it properly?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the SettingWithCopyWarning, a pesky little creature that loves to pop up when you're trying to add a new column to a sliced DataFrame. It's like trying to build a new bridge over a river, but the river is still flowing beneath you. The DataFrame you're working with, df2, is a copy of the original df, but it's a copy that doesn't know it's a copy. When you try to add a new column, pandas thinks you're trying to change the original df, which is not allowed. \n\nTo fix this, you need to tell pandas that you're working with a copy, not the original. You can do this by using the .copy() method when you slice your DataFrame. So, instead of df2 = df[df['age'] > 30], you should do df2 = df[df['age'] > 30].copy(). This way, pandas will know that you're working with a copy, and the SettingWithCopyWarning will disappear. Just remember, even though you're working with a copy, it's still a copy, so be careful not to accidentally change the original df. It's like dealing with a mischievous goblin - you have to be extra careful not to let it mess up your",
      "source": "target",
